course: dictionary
lesson: 973
title: Blurry and clear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Blurry means not clear or sharp, with fuzzy edges, so it is hard to see the details. Look at the words around it for clues. «Zara met a famous footballer and showed a blurry photo of a tall man's back.» The photo is fuzzy, so you cannot see the details, such as a face.
explain: The word can also describe memory. A blurry memory is one you cannot remember clearly. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it: «A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof» still makes sense if you say «A fuzzy or faraway photo is not real proof».
explain: Blurry is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as photo, edges, or vision. Its forms are blurry, blurrier and blurriest, which compare how fuzzy things are.
fact: Blurry means not clear or sharp, with fuzzy edges.
fact: A photo often looks blurry when the camera or the thing in it moves.
fact: «Blurry vision» means things look fuzzy to your eyes.
fact: Eyes can go blurry after swimming or crying, but if your eyesight suddenly goes blurry, or after you hit your head, tell an adult at once, because it can be a sign that you need a doctor.
fact: A blurry memory means you cannot remember clearly.
exercise: «Zara showed a blurry photo of a tall man's back.» What does blurry mean here? | *Not clear or sharp, with fuzzy edges | Brand new and never used | Very bright and colourful
exercise: «A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof.» Why is a blurry photo not good proof? | It is too big to carry | *You cannot see the details clearly | It has too many colours
exercise: Ella says, «My memory of the game is blurry.» What does she mean? | She remembers every single detail | She is watching the game right now | *She cannot remember it clearly
test: «AI can make pictures with strange hands and blurry writing.» What does blurry writing look like? | Large, bold and easy to read | *Fuzzy, with edges you cannot make out | Written in gold ink
test: «Eyes can go blurry after swimming or crying.» What does this suggest about the eyes? | *They cannot see things sharply for a while | They have turned a new colour | They are closed for ever
exam: Which sentence uses blurry in the same way as «a blurry photo»? | *The bus window was steamed up and the streetlights looked fuzzy | The sun was shining brightly over the beach | The teacher gave a clear answer to the question
